WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.159
In this lesson, we're going through the built-in tools that ship with the Cloud API.

2
00:00:04.719 --> 00:00:09.359
We'll cover what each tool does, where its boundaries are, and how the cost and latency

3
00:00:09.359 --> 00:00:14.880
profile compares to rolling your own custom tooling. If you're designing a Cloud-powered

4
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:21.280
system, this is the foundation you need. Let's get into it. Some capabilities you never have to

5
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:27.600
define at all, because Anthropic already did. A built-in tool is one that Anthropic has natively

6
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.759
wired into the Cloud API. You enable it through a typed parameter, rather than writing a schema

7
00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:40.639
yourself. WebSearch is the first built-in most architects reach for. It runs a constrained query,

8
00:00:40.639 --> 00:00:46.959
surfaces ranked snippets, and attaches source citations as structured metadata you can use

9
00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:52.959
downstream. The computer tool is the other big one, and it's an Anthropic schema client tool.

10
00:00:52.959 --> 00:00:58.639
It gives Cloud screenshot capability, mouse control, and keyboard input, but your own

11
00:00:58.639 --> 00:01:03.840
application controls the agent loop and runs it against a desktop environment you supply.

12
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:11.599
The built-in catalog splits into server tools and client tools, and that split matters more than any

13
00:01:11.599 --> 00:01:18.720
tool count. Server tools like WebSearch and Code Execution run on Anthropic's own infrastructure.

14
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.639
You get the results back directly with no handler code to write. Client tools use an

15
00:01:24.639 --> 00:01:30.480
Anthropic-published schema, but your application actually executes them. Text Editor views,

16
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.040
creates, and does string replacements and files. Bash runs shell commands, which is also how file

17
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.680
discovery happens, because the API ships no built-in glob or grep tool. The computer tool

18
00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:50.319
adds screenshot, mouse, and keyboard control. What all of them share is that you skip schema

19
00:01:50.319 --> 00:01:55.440
authoring. Anthropic has already done the prompt engineering and routing optimization,

20
00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.319
but client tools still run wherever your application runs, so where a tool runs

21
00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:06.879
decides what it can reach. The built-in versus custom distinction comes up constantly in API

22
00:02:06.879 --> 00:02:13.199
design conversations, so let's be precise about it. A built-in tool is defined and maintained

23
00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:19.759
by Anthropic. You get vetted prompts, optimized routing, and no schema work. A custom tool is

24
00:02:19.759 --> 00:02:26.720
something you define yourself through the standard tool use schema, typically backed by an MCP server

25
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.240
or an in-process function. The trade-off is straightforward. Built-ins are fast to adopt

26
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:38.240
and reliable for common tasks, but they have fixed scope. Custom tools give you full control over

27
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:45.039
behavior, schema, and integration, but you carry the maintenance burden. When what a built-in can do

28
00:02:45.039 --> 00:02:50.320
falls short of what your workflow actually needs, that gap is your signal to go custom.

29
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.720
Web Search has a few behavior details that trip people up. The model constructs the query

30
00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:02.399
internally based on the user's request and context. You don't pass a query string directly.

31
00:03:02.399 --> 00:03:07.839
The number of searches per request is capped by an optional max uses parameter, and each search

32
00:03:07.839 --> 00:03:14.000
counts as one use no matter how many results return. That's your lever for bounding cost. Rate

33
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:20.000
limits are a separate concern. If Claude exceeds them, the response still comes back successfully,

34
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.080
but it carries a web search tool result error object with an error code of too many requests

35
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:32.800
rather than silently returning fewer results. Every result comes with source citation metadata,

36
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.880
so Web Search isn't a raw search wrapper. It's an opinionated tool with its own query logic.

37
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.000
If you need precise query control, build a custom tool around the search service you want.

38
00:03:45.199 --> 00:03:51.600
The computer tool is the most powerful built-in and also the one most misunderstood. It's a client

39
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.960
tool, which means your application controls the agent loop and runs it against a desktop

40
00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.520
environment you supply. Screenshots capture visible screen state in that environment. Mouse

41
00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.759
control handles clicks, drags, and scrolls there. Keyboard input sends text and keystrokes to that

42
00:04:09.759 --> 00:04:15.919
same session. Here's the key point architects get wrong. There is no anthropic enforced sandbox.

43
00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.279
What the computer tool can reach is bounded by whatever environment you connect it to,

44
00:04:21.279 --> 00:04:26.720
and nothing more. If you point it at a machine with access to your host operating system,

45
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.679
or your private network, it can reach those. So isolation is your responsibility. Anthropic

46
00:04:33.679 --> 00:04:39.359
recommends running it inside a VM or container, and giving it only the access the task genuinely

47
00:04:39.359 --> 00:04:46.239
needs. Code execution gives Clod a place to run code, and the key detail is that the container

48
00:04:46.239 --> 00:04:52.399
persists. It lives for up to 30 days after creation, and you can reuse it across requests

49
00:04:52.399 --> 00:04:58.399
by passing its container ID back in. That means files and state you create can carry forward,

50
00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:03.679
not vanish between calls. The environment ships a fixed set of pre-installed libraries,

51
00:05:03.679 --> 00:05:09.839
like pandas, numpy, and matplotlib. You work with what's there because the container has no

52
00:05:09.839 --> 00:05:15.679
internet access. Outbound network requests are fully disabled for security, so you can't pull

53
00:05:15.679 --> 00:05:22.640
down new packages at runtime. Newer tool versions add REPL state persistence on top of the file

54
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.799
persistence. For system design, the takeaway is that code execution can hold state when you reuse

55
00:05:28.799 --> 00:05:34.559
the container, but plan around its fixed library set and its complete lack of network access.

56
00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:42.079
Cost and latency are real factors in built-in tool selection, and architects often underestimate

57
00:05:42.079 --> 00:05:48.559
how much they vary. Web search adds a full round trip for each query. If your workflow issues

58
00:05:48.559 --> 00:05:54.880
multiple searches per user request, that latency compounds. The computer tool is computationally

59
00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.239
expensive per screenshot cycle because every capture requires rendering, encoding, and

60
00:06:00.239 --> 00:06:06.559
transmission. Code execution carries a cold start overhead every time the container spins up, which

61
00:06:06.559 --> 00:06:13.279
matters in latency-sensitive applications. Custom tools backed by fast internal services can actually

62
00:06:13.279 --> 00:06:19.760
outperform built-ins on latency when the internal service is well optimized. The right design choice

63
00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:25.760
depends on your latency budget. For a background research task, web search latency is probably

64
00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:31.279
acceptable. For a real-time user interaction, you should measure before committing to a built-in.

65
00:06:32.239 --> 00:06:37.920
The built-in tools hand you working capability without writing a single schema, and that's the

66
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:44.640
appeal. What's easy to gloss over is where the work actually happens. Some run on Anthropx

67
00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.000
infrastructure and some run inside your own, and that line decides what your data touches.

68
00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:56.959
Keep in mind, each tool carries its own cost and latency shape, so it belongs in the design budget.

69
00:06:56.959 --> 00:07:01.519
That does it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next video.

